## Compact Binary Serialization

### Schema Definition

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message Person {
  int32 id = 1;
  string name = 2;
  string email = 3;
  repeated string tags = 4;
  Address address = 5;
}

message Address {
  string street = 1;
  string city = 2;
  int32 zip_code = 3;
}
```

### Serialization

```xiom
use xiom.protobuf;

fn main() -> Int {
  let person = Person{
    id: 1,
    name: "Alice",
    email: "alice@example.com",
    tags: ["developer", "xiom"],
    address: Some(Address{
      street: "123 Main St",
      city: "San Francisco",
      zip_code: 94105,
    }),
  };

  // Serialize to binary
  let bytes = protobuf::serialize(&person)?;
  io.println("Serialized size: " + bytes.len().to_str() + " bytes");
  // Typically 3-10x smaller than JSON equivalent

  // Deserialize
  let decoded: Person = protobuf::deserialize(&bytes)?;
  assert(decoded.name == "Alice");
  return 0;
}
```

### Schema Evolution

```protobuf
// Version 1
message User {
  int32 id = 1;
  string name = 2;
}

// Version 2 (add field)
message User {
  int32 id = 1;
  string name = 2;
  string email = 3;  // NEW — old data still readable
}

// Version 3 (deprecate field)
message User {
  int32 id = 1;
  string name = 2 [deprecated = true];
  string username = 3;  // Replaced 'name'
}
```

### Wire Format

```
Protobuf binary encoding:
Field: tag (field_number << 3 | wire_type), value

Example:
  id = 42
  → tag: 08  (1 << 3 | 0 = field 1, varint)
  → value: 2A  (42 as varint)
  → 2 bytes total

vs JSON: {"id":42} = 7 bytes
```

### Comparison with JSON

| Feature | JSON | Protobuf |
|---------|------|----------|
| Format | Text | Binary |
| Size | Large | 3-10x smaller |
| Parse speed | Medium | Fast |
| Human-readable | Yes | No |
| Schema | Optional | Required |
| Schema evolution | Manual | Built-in |
| Language support | Universal | Proto definitions |

